{"hash":"3dd573a1f26e4d9d","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":91.455,"said":"As you walk along this lane, listen to the story the Shiva Purana tells of how the Lord came to live here. Long ago the Vindhya mountains, the great range that runs across the middle of India, were proud. When the wandering sage Narada came to visit, Vindhya welcomed him with honour, and then boasted that he lacked nothing at all. Narada replied gently that Mount Meru was loftier still, and was counted among the gods. The words stung. Vindhya resolved to win Shiva’s grace. He came here, to Omkara, shaped a linga out of earth, and for six months worshipped Shiva without leaving his place. At last Shiva appeared, pleased, and granted his wish. Then the gods and sages begged Shiva to stay here for the good of all the worlds, and he agreed. The Purana says he remained in two forms: as Omkara, present in the sacred syllable itself, and as Parameshvara, risen from that earthen linga. The temple’s tradition knows these two as Omkareshwar on the island and Amareshwar, or Mamleshwar, across the river; the Stotram names them together, ‘Omkaram Amaleshwaram’. Now the path goes down toward the water. Can you hear the river?\n\nDid you know? An inscription of 1063 at Amareshvara lists five great lingas: Amareshvara, Omkara, Avimukta of Varanasi, Kedara, and Mahakala of Ujjain.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1129},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1145,"end":1282}],"words":[[0.098,0.192,0,2],[0.302,0.108,3,6],[0.423,0.252,7,11],[0.687,0.337,12,17],[1.036,0.228,18,22],[1.276,0.373,23,27],[1.877,0.349,29,35],[2.237,0.096,36,38],[2.346,0.096,39,42],[2.454,0.433,43,48],[2.899,0.108,49,52],[3.019,0.361,53,58],[3.391,0.469,59,65],[3.872,0.325,66,71],[4.209,0.132,72,74],[4.353,0.276,75,78],[4.641,0.096,79,82],[4.75,0.325,83,87],[5.086,0.276,88,92],[5.375,0.108,93,95],[5.495,0.228,96,100],[5.735,0.3,101,105],[6.935,0.397,107,111],[7.344,0.252,112,115],[7.608,0.12,116,119],[7.74,0.397